body:
The issue is right [here](https://github.com/eslint/eslint/blob/dfcfcf2a6437a3c16219781ca07028df3c786d05/lib/config.js#L231-238):

``` javascript
    if (this.useSpecificConfig) {
        debug("Merging command line config file");
        config = util.mergeConfigs(config, this.useSpecificConfig);

        if (this.useSpecificConfig.env) {
            config = util.mergeConfigs(config, createEnvironmentConfig(this.useSpecificConfig.env, this.options.reset));
        }
    }
```

since the default environment rules are merged after the actual rules in the config file.

The fix is simple, but it's not obvious to me how to test it. I tried adding a `"no-mixed-requires"` rule for all tests related to CLI config files, but the bug does not show up. This is because all of them use `reset: true`, which prevents the undesired overwrite.

I guess that this is done to avoid the burden of comparing with the actual default rules per environment. What would be the correct way to proceed then? Would mocking the environment default rules do the trick?

Same issue here _(having defined under `~/.eslintrc`)_ but the only way to make the warning go away is to define within the document itself.

```
/*eslint-env node*/
/*eslint no-mixed-requires: 0*/
…
```
